== Strings: what nearly every program needs (canon r3.64 module pairs:
== Std.X holds a subject's fundamental operations, Std.XUtils the
== conveniences). They are compiler intrinsics, visible in a program that
== uses Std.Strings or Std.StrUtils, so their names stay free elsewhere.
== Concatenation needs no module: A + B joins two Strings everywhere.
==
==   Length(S String) Integer                  == characters (Unicode code points)
==   ByteLength(S String) Integer              == UTF-8 bytes
==   Substr(S String, Start Integer, Count Integer) String
==                                             == Count characters from Start (1 = first)
==   Position(Sub String, S String) Integer    == where Sub first starts in S, 0 when absent
==   UpperCase(S String) String                == Unicode case mapping, any language
==   LowerCase(S String) String
==   CharAt(S String, I Integer) Char         == the character at position I (char-values)
==
== Positions count characters and start at 1. A position or count outside
== the String stops the program with "string index out of range" (CharAt
== needs 1 <= I <= Length(S)): Substr
== needs 1 <= Start <= Length(S) + 1, Count >= 0 and Start + Count - 1 <=
== Length(S). Case mappings take one character to one (ß stays ß).
